      "seed": 899308197,
      "version": 1716,
      "versionNonce": 557491733,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1793078330169,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Design an Event Forwarding Framework supporting batching, failures, and independent processing\n\n1. Functional Requirements : \n    1. Event ingestion: Accept events from multiple producers.\n    2. Batching: Group events before forwarding to reduce network overhead.\n    3. Independent delivery: Forward events to multiple destinations independently.\n    4. Failure handling: Retry failed deliveries and support a dead-letter queue (DLQ).\n    5. Monitoring: Track delivery status, failures, and retries.\n\n2. Non - Functional Requirements : \n    1. High Availability (99.99%) for the ingestion API.\n    2. At-least-once delivery guarantee (zero dropped events).\n    3. Scalability: Handle increasing event volumes using horizontal scaling.\n    4. Reliability: Avoid losing acknowledged events.\n    5. Fault isolation: One destination's failure must not block others.\n\n3. Back of the envelope estimation : \n    1. 10K clients * 100k events = 10,000 QPS\n    2. Peak QPS = 3 * 10,000 = 30,000 QPS\n    3. Delivery Avg = 10,000 QPS * 10 = 100,000 QPS\n    4. Peak Delivery = 3 * 100,000 = 300,00 QPS\n    5. Storage = 10,000 QPS * 10 * 5KB = 500 MB\n\n4. Data Models and Entities :\n    1. user / client = - client_id, events, event_type - PostgreSQL\n    2. event = event_id, event_type, client_id, payload, created_at - Cassandra - High write throughput\n    3. destination= destination_id, event_type, endpoint_url, status - PostgreSQL\n    4. delivery = delivery_id, event_id, destination_id, event_type, endpoint_url, status, attempt_count - Cassandra\n\n5. API Designing :\n    1. Ingest : POST : /api/v1/evnent: {\"event_id\" :...... , \"event\" : [],[],...[] , \"event_type\" : .... , \"created_at\" : ....}\n    2. Register : POST : /api/v1/destinations : { \"destination_id\" : .... , \"event_type\" : ....., \"endpoint_url\" : ...., \"status\": ...}\n    3. Check status : GET /api/v1/events/{event_id}/deliveries : { \"event_id\": \"123\", \"destinations\": [ {\"target\": \"Mixpanel\", \"status\": \"SUCCESS\",\n\"attempts\": 1}, {\"target\": \"Billing\", \"status\": \"RETRYING\", \"attempts\": 2} ] }\n6. High level Diagram: \n",
      "fontSize": 20.497868028269774,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Design an Event Forwarding Framework supporting batching, failures, and independent processing\n\n1. Functional Requirements : \n    1. Event ingestion: Accept events from multiple producers.\n    2. Batching: Group events before forwarding to reduce network overhead.\n    3. Independent delivery: Forward events to multiple destinations independently.\n    4. Failure handling: Retry failed deliveries and support a dead-letter queue (DLQ).\n    5. Monitoring: Track delivery status, failures, and retries.\n\n2. Non - Functional Requirements : \n    1. High Availability (99.99%) for the ingestion API.\n    2. At-least-once delivery guarantee (zero dropped events).\n    3. Scalability: Handle increasing event volumes using horizontal scaling.\n    4. Reliability: Avoid losing acknowledged events.\n    5. Fault isolation: One destination's failure must not block others.\n\n3. Back of the envelope estimation : \n    1. 10K clients * 100k events = 10,000 QPS\n    2. Peak QPS = 3 * 10,000 = 30,000 QPS\n    3. Delivery Avg = 10,000 QPS * 10 = 100,000 QPS\n    4. Peak Delivery = 3 * 100,000 = 300,00 QPS\n    5. Storage = 10,000 QPS * 10 * 5KB = 500 MB\n\n4. Data Models and Entities :\n    1. user / client = - client_id, events, event_type - PostgreSQL\n    2. event = event_id, event_type, client_id, payload, created_at - Cassandra - High write throughput\n    3. destination= destination_id, event_type, endpoint_url, status - PostgreSQL\n    4. delivery = delivery_id, event_id, destination_id, event_type, endpoint_url, status, attempt_count - Cassandra\n\n5. API Designing :\n    1. Ingest : POST : /api/v1/evnent: {\"event_id\" :...... , \"event\" : [],[],...[] , \"event_type\" : .... , \"created_at\" : ....}\n    2. Register : POST : /api/v1/destinations : { \"destination_id\" : .... , \"event_type\" : ....., \"endpoint_url\" : ...., \"status\": ...}\n    3. Check status : GET /api/v1/events/{event_id}/deliveries : { \"event_id\": \"123\", \"destinations\": [ {\"target\": \"Mixpanel\", \"status\": \"SUCCESS\", \"attempts\": 1}, {\"target\": \"Billing\", \"status\": \"RETRYING\", \"attempts\": 2} ] }\n6. High level Diagram: \n",
